 Actually doc 17.12 says "Object ID length max 63 ASCII bytes". So an ID lo=
nger than 63 should be rejected. But with what code? Possibly PBT_E_TOKEN_T=
OO_LONG (token/string/identifier exceeds hard limit). Let me make token har=
d limit =3D 4096 generally, but for IDs specifically enforce 63 (could be P=
BT_E_TOKEN_TOO_LONG). To be safe, I'll set a general TOKEN_MAX =3D 4096 and=
 ID_MAX check separately yielding PBT_E_TOKEN_TOO_LONG. The id_too_long.pbt=
 (615 bytes) =E2=80=94 need to see its content. Let me check it later. For =
now, I'll set TOKEN_HARD_LIMIT =3D 8192? But then string_too_long (12731) &=
gt; 8192 =E2=86=92 TOKEN_TOO_LONG. Good. And id_too_long would be caught by=
 ID length 63 check. But if I set token limit at 8192, an ID of e.g. 200 ch=
ars would pass token limit but fail ID length. I'll handle ID length explic=
itly (PBT_E_TOKEN_TOO_LONG).</p>
<p>Hmm, but actually the malformed file naming: id_too_long, string_too_lon=
g, line_too_long, token... there's no separate "token_too_long" file but th=
e code exists. Let me map:</p>
