e>. I can change the parse helpers so they DON'T return PBT_E_TYPE but... n=
o, numeric type errors SHOULD be PBT_E_TYPE in many cases (wrong_scalar_typ=
e fixture expects PBT_E_TYPE).</p>
<p>Wait =E2=80=94 wrong_scalar_type expects PBT_E_TYPE. So when a field get=
s a non-numeric value (wrong scalar type), parse_f64 returns PBT_E_TYPE, an=
d we want to report PBT_E_TYPE. So propagating the real code is correct: pa=
rse_f64 returns PBT_E_TYPE for "abc", we report PBT_E_TYPE. For "nan" retur=
ns NONFINITE, report NONFINITE. For "1e999" returns NUMERIC_OVERFLOW, repor=
t that.</p>
<p>So the fix <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">report(diag, rc, ln, key)</cod=
e> is universally correct. The only wrong thing currently is hardcoding PBT=
_E_TYPE. And the <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">malformed_vector</code> cas=
e (gravity=3D(0,) =E2=86=92 parse_vec2 returns PBT_E_TYPE =E2=86=92 reporte=
d as PBT_E_TYPE =E2=9C=93). Good.</p>
<p>So I'll change all <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">else report(diag, PBT_=
E_TYPE, ln, key)</code> =E2=86=92 capture rc and report rc. And <code class=
=3D"cr-inline-code">else report(diag, PBT_E_SYNTAX, ln, "unknown ... field"=
)</code> should stay PBT_E_SYNTAX for truly unknown fields (those are sync/=
typo in field names =E2=86=92 syntax error). Actually unknown field names: =
doc might treat as warning, but my code reports PBT_E_SYNTAX. The oracle do=
