<p>Better approach: Instead of a mask, after parsing the event, I can detec=
t gaps by checking <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">actions[a].type =3D=3D AC=
T_ADD_SCORE</code> AND "was it explicitly set?". The fundamental problem is=
 distinguishing unset from set-to-ADD_SCORE.</p>
<p>Cleanest robust solution: Add a sentinel. Since ACT_COUNT is the "invali=
d" value but it's 12 not 0, I should explicitly initialize the actions arra=
y to ACT_COUNT after memset. Then unset slots remain ACT_COUNT and the orig=
inal check works!</p>
<p>But the Event struct is memset to 0 (ACT_ADD_SCORE) in the parser. If I =
instead initialize <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">cur_event.actions[a].type=
 =3D ACT_COUNT</code> for all a after memset, then unset stays ACT_COUNT. T=
he post-parse loop <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">if (ev-&gt;actions[a].typ=
e =3D=3D ACT_COUNT)</code> works perfectly.</p>
<p>Let me do that. After <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">memset(&amp;cur_eve=
nt,0,sizeof(cur_event))</code> in the <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">[event=
 ...]</code> branch, loop and set all actions to ACT_COUNT. Since PB_MAX_AC=
TIONS=3D256, that's a 256-iteration loop =E2=80=94 fine, only on event sect=
ion start.</p>
<p>Actually, I can initialize the whole actions array once. Let me add afte=
