 1.29em;"><p>All 30 oracle cases pass now (28 malformed + 2 valid).</p>
<p>Now I need to continue with the remaining work. This is a HUGE task. Let=
 me reconsider the scope and be strategic about what's realistically achiev=
able and verifiable in this environment.</p>
<p>The full task per the spec package includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Scene writer (v2 serialization, round-trip)</li>
<li>Deterministic physics engine</li>
<li>Gameplay/scoring/events/multiball/nudge/tilt</li>
<li>Replay record/replay/validate + detcompare</li>
<li>Software renderer + PNG writer (no zlib)</li>
<li>Editor (edit mode) + Win32 layer</li>
<li>7 engineering utilities + headless CLI</li>
<li>=E2=89=A5420 tests</li>
<li>Visual evidence (38 static + 25 transition) as real PNG frames</li>
<li>Release artifacts</li>
</ul>
<p>This is an enormous amount. Given the constraints, I should focus on del=
ivering a coherent, genuinely working core that satisfies the most importan=
t mandatory requirements, with real (not mocked) implementations. But I mus=
t be honest about what passes vs what doesn't (the user explicitly required=
 transparency).</p>
<p>Given the realistic limits, the most valuable and verifiable deliverable=
 is:</p>
<ol>
<li>The parser (DONE, fully verified against oracle - 30/30).</li>
<li>Scene writer + round-trip (production code, testable in headless).</li>
<li>A deterministic physics engine (production, testable headless).</li>
<li>Replay + determinism harness.</li>
<li>A headless test suite with =E2=89=A5420 assertions (or as many as feasi=
ble) covering parser, writer, physics determinism, replay.</li>
<li>The engineering utilities (scenecheck, simcheck, replaycheck, detcompar=
e, etc.) as real CLIs.</li>
<li>PNG writer (no zlib) for visual evidence.</li>
<li>Release artifacts.</li>
</ol>
<p>The GUI/Win32/editor parts can't be visually verified in this sandbox (n=
o display), so I must be transparent that GUI-behavior gates are not run, w=
hile the production core (shared with GUI) is fully implemented and tested =
headless.</p>
<p>However, this is a massive undertaking. Given the context, I think the b=
est approach is to build it incrementally but efficiently, writing real cod=
e. Let me be pragmatic: I'll proceed to implement the scene writer next (ro=
und-trip test), then physics, then replay, then a comprehensive headless te=
st harness, then the utilities, then PNG/visual evidence, then release docs=
.</p>
<p>But realistically, doing ALL of this with full fidelity in one session i=
s extremely large. Let me prioritize and be honest. I'll build a solid, gen=
uinely functional core and clearly document status.</p>
<p>Let me think about what the spec actually requires vs. what I can verify=
. Given the time, let me focus on producing real, working, testable code fo=
r:</p>
<ul>
<li>Parser =E2=9C=93</li>
<li>Writer (round-trip)</li>
<li>Physics (deterministic, dt=3D1/240)</li>
<li>Replay</li>
<li>Tests (headless, as many as feasible =E2=80=94 aim for the floor where =
practical)</li>
<li>Utilities</li>
<li>PNG writer</li>
<li>Release artifacts</li>
</ul>
<p>And implement the Win32/renderer/editor as real code too (so the GUI bui=
ld compiles), but mark GUI-behavior gates as "implemented, not visually ver=
ified in headless sandbox."</p>
<p>Let me proceed efficiently. First, let me check the spec docs to underst=
and the exact data formats and requirements. I should read the key spec doc=
s: doc 06 (scene format), doc 15 (physics), doc 07 (replay), doc 31 (error =
codes), doc 12 (release gates), doc 28 (tests). Let me read several in para=
llel to ground my implementation.</p>
