<li><code class=3D"cr-inline-code">legacy_editor_full_table_v1.pbt</code>: =
roundtrip mismatch in fingerprint (text equal but fp differ) =E2=80=94 beca=
use v1 migration adds a "gameplay" layer and assigns objects; but wait, it =
says text_equal=3D1 (the idempotence holds, writing twice gives same text).=
 The fp mismatch is between s1 (parsed v1 =E2=86=92 migrated to v2 with gam=
eplay layer, nudge defaults) and s2 (parsed the written v2). Why would fing=
erprints differ if the written text is identical?</li>
</ol>
<p>Fingerprint mismatch with identical text means the fingerprint depends o=
n something not captured in the written text OR ordering differs. Actually =
wait =E2=80=94 text_equal=3D1 means t1=3D=3Dt2 byte-identical (idempotent).=
 But fp1 (from s1, the v1-parsed-then-migrated scene) !=3D fp2 (from s2, th=
e v2 parse of t1). Since t1=3D=3Dt2, parsing t1 should give s2, and s1 was =
parsed from the original v1 file. The difference: s1 is parsed from v1 whic=
h gets migrated (adds gameplay layer with order=3D0, assigns all objects to=
 it). s2 is parsed from t1 (v2) which has the gameplay layer explicitly.</p=
>
<p>Why would fingerprints differ? Possibly because the fingerprint hashes <=
code class=3D"cr-inline-code">layer.order</code> and the migrated v1 layer =
gets order=3D0, but in t1 the writer emits order=3D0 too. Hmm. Or the finge=
rprint hashes something order-dependent.</p>
<p>Actually =E2=80=94 maybe the issue is that in v1 migration, objects get =
assigned to "gameplay" layer, but the writer emits them with <code class=3D=
"cr-inline-code">layer =3D gameplay</code>. When re-parsed, same. So s1 and=
 s2 should be identical in content. Unless the v1 scene has objects NOT ass=
igned because... Actually the migration loops over all objects and sets lay=
er=3D"gameplay". And adds a gameplay layer. So s1 has layer_count=3D1 (game=
play) and all objects layer=3Dgameplay. s2 parsed from t1 has the same. So =
why fp differs?</p>
<p>Wait =E2=80=94 maybe the fingerprint hashes <code class=3D"cr-inline-cod=
e">o-&gt;order</code> (authored stable order) and that's preserved. Or mayb=
e it hashes the objects in array order; both preserved. Hmm.</p>
<p>Let me think: text_equal=3D1 means t1 =3D=3D t2. So writing s1 gives t1,=
 writing s2 gives t2, and they're equal. That means s1 and s2 serialize ide=
ntically. If they serialize identically, their canonical content is identic=
al. But fingerprint is computed BEFORE writing (fp1 on s1, fp2 on s2). If s=
1 and s2 serialize identically but have different fingerprints, then the fi=
ngerprint must depend on a field NOT serialized by the writer.</p>
<p>What's serialized? All the writer fields. What's in Scene but not serial=
ized? <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">format</code> (1 vs 2!), <code class=
=3D"cr-inline-code">has_seed</code>, and per-object <code class=3D"cr-inlin=
e-code">order</code> (serialized? Let me check =E2=80=94 writer for objects=
 does NOT emit <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">order</code>! Let me verify. =
In scene_write, for objects I emit type-specific fields + layer + locked, b=
ut NOT <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">order</code>. The <code class=3D"cr-i=
nline-code">order</code> field is <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">authored s=
