Re-parse: DRAIN debug_visible =3D 0 (default). So both 0. Same.</p>
<p>Hmm. Let me look for a field hashed in fingerprint but NOT in writer, OR=
 hashed differently (e.g., 1 byte vs 4 bytes) causing alignment difference.=
.. no, fnv is order-sensitive but each update is independent.</p>
<p>Wait =E2=80=94 the key insight: the fingerprint hashes <code class=3D"cr=
-inline-code">fnv1a64_update(&amp;h, &amp;o-&gt;u.spawn.has_ball_radius, 1)=
;</code> (1 byte) and then <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">hash_double(&amp;=
h, o-&gt;u.spawn.ball_radius);</code>. For a spawn WITHOUT ball_radius: s1 =
has_ball_radius=3D0, ball_radius=3D0. Writer omits ball_radius. s2: has_bal=
l_radius=3D0, ball_radius=3D0. Same.</p>
<p>Let me look at the capsule case (142-150): hashes start,end,thickness,re=
stitution,friction,enabled. The writer emits MORE fields for subtypes. But =
those extra fields aren't hashed, so they don't cause fp difference. And th=
e hashed fields ARE emitted. So same.</p>
<p>Hmm, but what about the order of fields within a struct hash? It's a fix=
ed sequence per type. Same for s1 and s2.</p>
<p>Wait =E2=80=94 could it be that the migration changes object ORDER vs re=
-parse? No, order isn't hashed.</p>
<p>Let me reconsider: maybe the difference is in how many objects or the la=
yer. Both have obj=3D10/10, layer=3D1/1 for legacy. So identical counts.</p=
>
<p>Let me look more carefully at ALL object types' hash vs writer. The fing=
erprint for BUMPER hashes base_score via <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">siz=
