t: 1.29em;"><p>Key findings:</p>
<ol>
<li>The function is <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">scene_write(const Scene =
*s)</code> =E2=86=92 returns <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">char *</code>. =
NOT <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">write_scene</code>. And there's <code cl=
ass=3D"cr-inline-code">scene_write_file</code>. So in editor.c, <code class=
=3D"cr-inline-code">write_scene(s, &amp;buf, &amp;n, &amp;d)</code> calls a=
 non-existent function. I need to replace <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">wr=
ite_scene</code> usage with <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">scene_write</cod=
e>.</li>
</ol>
<p>The editor uses <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">write_scene</code> in:</p=
>
<ul>
<li><code class=3D"cr-inline-code">history_push</code>: <code class=3D"cr-i=
nline-code">write_scene(s, &amp;buf, &amp;n, &amp;d);</code> =E2=86=92 <cod=
e class=3D"cr-inline-code">buf =3D scene_write(s);</code> then <code class=
=3D"cr-inline-code">n =3D strlen(buf)</code>.</li>
<li><code class=3D"cr-inline-code">save_scene</code>: <code class=3D"cr-inl=
ine-code">write_scene(&amp;a-&gt;scene,&amp;buf,&amp;n,&amp;d);</code> =E2=
=86=92 <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">buf =3D scene_write(&amp;a-&gt;scene)=
; n =3D strlen(buf);</code>. Then writes buf to file.</li>
</ul>
<p>But <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">scene_write</code> doesn't take a Dia=
gList*. So I remove the diag param. Also need to free buf with free().</p>
<p>Let me check: does <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">scene_write</code> exi=
st and is declared in scene_write.h =E2=80=94 yes: <code class=3D"cr-inline=
-code">char *scene_write(const Scene *s);</code>. Good. And it's in scene_w=
rite.h which editor includes. But the earlier "implicit declaration of func=
tion 'write_scene'" =E2=80=94 because write_scene doesn't exist. After rena=
ming to scene_write, the declaration is visible. Good.</p>
<ol start=3D"2">
<li><code class=3D"cr-inline-code">a_bmi</code> is declared <code class=3D"=
cr-inline-code">static BmiRGB a_bmi;</code> at line 494, AFTER <code class=
=3D"cr-inline-code">resize_framebuffer</code> (line ~495) and after <code c=
lass=3D"cr-inline-code">blit_to_window</code> (line 484). For a <code class=
=3D"cr-inline-code">static</code> variable at file scope, it IS visible thr=
oughout the translation unit (C scoping for file-scope identifiers doesn't =
depend on order). So why "undeclared"?</li>
</ol>
<p>Wait =E2=80=94 the error said line 484:73 <code class=3D"cr-inline-code"=
>'a_bmi' undeclared</code>. But there's also <code class=3D"cr-inline-code"=
>resize_framebuffer</code> at line 505 using <code class=3D"cr-inline-code"=
>a_bmi.h.biSize</code>. If <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">a_bmi</code> were=
 truly undeclared, resize_framebuffer would also fail. But the error list o=
nly showed line 484 for a_bmi. Hmm. Possibly because the error list is trun=
cated (head -30). Actually the grep output for errors captured all "error:"=
 lines (it ended at "EXIT=3D1" lines). The a_bmi error appeared once. Maybe=
 resize_framebuffer's usage didn't error because... no, it should. Unless t=
he compiler only reports first reference. GCC reports each use. But maybe t=
he <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">static BmiRGB a_bmi;</code> definition pr=
ovides the declaration for subsequent uses, and the FIRST use (line 484, bl=
it_to_window) is BEFORE the definition (line 494), causing "undeclared" for=
 that forward reference. Wait, but static file-scope variables ARE visible =
before their definition in C. Actually NO =E2=80=94 for identifiers with in=
ternal linkage (static) at file scope, they have file scope and are visible=
 from the point of declaration to the end of the translation unit, NOT befo=
re. Wait, that's the rule: a declaration is visible from its point of decla=
ration onward within its scope. For a file-scope static, the scope is the w=
hole file, but visibility starts at the declaration. So uses BEFORE line 49=
4 (the definition/declaration) are NOT visible =E2=86=92 "undeclared". That=
 explains it! Unlike functions (which have implicit declaration rules), a s=
tatic variable used before its declaration is an error in C (actually, C re=
quires a declaration before use; a tentative definition at line 494 declare=
s it, so uses before 494 are undeclared).</p>
<p>So the fix: move <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">static BmiRGB a_bmi;</co=
de> (and the BmiRGB typedef) to BEFORE <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">blit_=
to_window</code>, i.e., near the top of the file (after includes). Let me m=
ove the typedef and the static var up. I'll place them right after the incl=
udes / WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN, before APP_TITLE.</p>
