<p>Note: event order=E2=80=94events at the same step; my scan processes in =
array order. The recorder stores in order. For determinism of reconstructio=
n, the down/up semantics: when a DOWN event occurs at step i, from that ste=
p onward pressed. My loop sets pL based on latest event &lt;=3D i. That mat=
ches: an UP at step j means from step j onward released. But the original r=
un called sim_input with L=3D(i%20&lt;3) each step directly (absolute state=
), while the recorder captures EDGES (transitions). So reconstructing absol=
ute state from edges: at each step, pressed =3D (number of DOWN - number of=
 UP for that button up to and including this step) is odd/even... Actually =
edge model: DOWN sets pressed=3D1, UP sets pressed=3D0. Starting pressed=3D=
0. So pressed state at step i =3D state after processing all events with st=
ep&lt;=3Di in order. That's exactly what my loop computes (process events i=
n order, set pL=3D1 on DOWN, 0 on UP). And events have distinct steps gener=
ally, but multiple events could share a step; order matters. The recorder s=
hould emit edges in step order. Good enough.</p>
<p>But there's subtlety: sim_input captures edges (rising). In the original=
 run, I called sim_input every step with absolute L. The recorder only reco=
rds when L transitions 0=E2=86=921 (rising) as RPL_L_FLIPPER_DOWN, and 1=E2=
=86=920 as UP. So the events capture transitions. Reconstructing absolute f=
rom transitions and feeding sim_input each step should reproduce the same i=
nternal edge detection =E2=86=92 same result. Good.</p>
<p>This is a solid replay determinism test. I'll compare fp_original (from =
run_with_recorder) vs fp_replay (apply_replay) =E2=80=94 they should be equ=
al.</p>
