de class=3D"cr-inline-code">PBT_E_LAYER</code>. So now the <code class=3D"c=
r-inline-code">layer</code> field itself is causing PBT_E_LAYER. I set <cod=
e class=3D"cr-inline-code">o.layer =3D "1"</code>, but the parser expects l=
ayer to be a valid layer name and "1" might not be a valid layer (layers mi=
ght need to be defined in the [layers] section, or layer names have constra=
ints). So PBT_E_LAYER means the layer value isn't valid (not in defined lay=
ers, or wrong format).</p>
<p>This confirms the synthetic-object approach is fragile. The robust fix p=
er the summary: use real fixture objects. Let me rewrite section 6.</p>
<p>The plan: For each object type t (0..OBJ_COUNT-1), find a real object of=
 that type in the parsed fixtures. Build a minimal scene, add THAT real obj=
ect (copy it), scene_write, reparse, verify count=3D=3D1 and type=3D=3Dt. T=
his avoids all the default-field issues because the real object already has=
 valid values.</p>
<p>But there's a subtlety: the real object belongs to a fixture scene that =
has its own layers/groups defined. When we extract just the object and put =
it in a minimal scene without those layers defined, the layer validation mi=
ght fail (PBT_E_LAYER). So I need the scene to also include the layers the =
object references, OR clear the object's layer/group to valid defaults that=
 exist in the minimal scene.</p>
<p>Hmm. Let me check how layer validation works. PBT_E_LAYER =E2=80=94 when=
 is it triggered? Let me look at the parse validation for layer.</p>
<p>Actually, a simpler robust approach: instead of round-trip via a minimal=
 scene, I can round-trip via the ORIGINAL fixture scene. For each object in=
 each fixture, take the full scene, remove all objects except that one, wri=
te, reparse, verify the single object type matches. That way layers/groups =
are preserved (the original scene defines them). And the object is real.</p=
>
<p>But wait =E2=80=94 removing all but one object might leave the scene wit=
h a layer defined but the object references it =E2=80=94 that's fine. And t=
he table-level fields come from the real fixture (valid). So reparse should=
 succeed.</p>
<p>Even simpler: for each fixture, for each object, verify that scene_write=
 of the WHOLE scene round-trips (already covered in section 1). For the per=
-type coverage, I can verify: collect the set of object types present acros=
s all fixtures; for each type, find a fixture+object; create a scene that i=
s a copy of the real fixture but with only that object; write+reparse; asse=
rt obj_count=3D=3D1 and type matches.</p>
<p>Let me implement that. I need to:</p>
<ol>
<li>Parse each fixture.</li>
<li>For each object in fixture, record (fixture_index, obj_index, type).</l=
i>
<li>Group by type. For each type 0..OBJ_COUNT-1, pick the first object of t=
hat type found.</li>
<li>Build a new Scene =3D copy of the fixture scene, but remove all objects=
 except the chosen one. Actually easier: create a fresh scene via scene_ini=
t, then manually copy table-level fields from the source scene, and add jus=
t the one object. But copying the whole scene struct is complex.</li>
</ol>
<p>Alternative: Add a helper to copy scene. Or: After parsing source scene,=
 iterate objects and remove all except the target. There's <code class=3D"c=
r-inline-code">scene_remove_object_at</code>. I can call it repeatedly.</p>
<p>But the table-level layer definitions: if I keep the source scene's laye=
rs but only one object, that's fine. The object's layer must reference a de=
fined layer. Since I keep the source's layers, it's valid.</p>
<p>Let me write the new section 6:</p>
