<p>Wait, but <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">OBJ_COUNT</code> =E2=80=94 is i=
t defined? Let me check. The original code used <code class=3D"cr-inline-co=
de">for(int t=3D0;t&lt;OBJ_COUNT;t++)</code>, so OBJ_COUNT must be defined.=
 Good.</p>
<p>But there's a concern: <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">scene_remove_objec=
t_at</code> removes by index and shifts down. If I remove from the end (des=
cending), and <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">keep</code> is fixed, removing=
 oi &gt; keep shifts lower indices down by one but keep index unaffected (s=
ince keep &lt; oi). And removing oi &lt; keep would shift keep down. Since =
I remove from highest to lowest and skip <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">kee=
p</code>, removing an index below keep would shift keep's position. But I i=
terate descending, so when I remove an index below keep, keep hasn't been p=
rocessed yet as a removal, but keep's actual position shifts. Hmm, that's a=
 bug: after removing index 5 (keep=3D3), indices &gt;=3D5 shift down, but k=
eep=3D3 is unaffected. When I later remove index 2 (which is &lt; keep=3D3)=
, the object at keep (originally index 3) shifts to index 2! So <code class=
=3D"cr-inline-code">keep</code> must be tracked by the object's identity, n=
ot index.</p>
<p>Better: remove all objects except the one. Simplest: build a fresh list.=
 Let me do:</p>
