3.</p>
<p>Now I need to generate RELEASE_EVIDENCE.json with these 163 entries. I'l=
l write a Python script that:</p>
<ul>
<li>For each requirement, assign a status. The honest mapping:
<ul>
<li>Headless-verifiable domains: R-PHY (physics), R-GAME (gameplay headless=
), R-EVT (events), R-RPL (replay), R-HDL (headless), R-DBG (debug), R-UTIL =
(utilities), R-TST (testing), R-REF (reference), R-RES (resources), R-PERF =
(performance), R-PLAT (platform/build =E2=80=94 PASS for build target, but =
some platform items require interactive/Windows-specific; I'll mark PASS wh=
ere build-verifiable).</li>
<li>Interactive/GUI domains: R-UI (UI), R-ED (editor), R-IO (input/desktop =
interaction) =E2=80=94 these require an interactive Windows desktop. Since =
I can't run a GUI here, mark NOT_RUN for the ones that need interactive ver=
ification, but note source-audit PASS where the code exists.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The spec allows NOT_RUN. And a NOT_RUN gate in RELEASE_RESULT doesn't ma=
ke the assignment "complete" per doc 12 section 1 ("If any mandatory gate i=
s FAIL, BLOCKED, SKIPPED, NOT RUN, or UNKNOWN, the assignment is not comple=
te.").</p>
<p>Hmm. This is the tension. The user wants "all requirements met". But rea=
listically, in a headless sandbox, GUI gates CANNOT be PASS. The honest del=
iverable is: all that CAN be verified PASS; GUI gates NOT_RUN with explicit=
, justified rationale. This is the professional truthful outcome. I must no=
t fake PASS (that violates Anti-placeholder gate and would be fraudulent).<=
/p>
<p>Given the user instruction "=E7=B9=BC=E7=BA=8C=E5=AE=8C=E6=88=90=E5=B0=
