padding-left: 1.29em;"><p>The DBG_CATS didn't print to stderr. Wait, the re=
direct <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">2&gt;&amp;1 1&gt;/dev/null</code> =E2=
=80=94 order matters: <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">2&gt;&amp;1</code> fir=
st duplicates stderr to current stdout (terminal), then <code class=3D"cr-i=
nline-code">1&gt;/dev/null</code> redirects stdout to nul. So stderr still =
goes to terminal. But grep DBG found nothing. So DBG_CATS wasn't printed. T=
hat means the program exited BEFORE reaching that fprintf? But it printed t=
he JSON summary (to stdout, captured by 2&gt;&amp;1). The JSON is printed a=
t the emit block, and DBG_CATS is AFTER the g_fail block which is after the=
 emit. So DBG_CATS should print. Unless the program returned before? The em=
it block prints, then <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">if(g_fail)</code> (fal=
se), then DBG_CATS. It should print. But grep found nothing.</p>
<p>Wait, maybe stderr of tests.exe is being captured differently. The build=
 might have failed (TESTS BUILD OK not shown). Let me check /tmp/b.log. Act=
ually the first command <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">bash tools/build_tes=
ts.sh &gt;/tmp/b.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code> then <code class=3D"cr-inline-code"=
>./build/tests.exe 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep DBG_CATS</code> returned empty. Then =
the second command shows the JSON and the two child-tool lines to stderr (P=
ARSE_OK, frame written) =E2=80=94 those go to nul now! Wait, no =E2=80=94 i=
n the second command <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">./build/tests.exe 2&gt;=
&amp;1 | tail -3</code> shows <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">frame written<=
/code> and <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">PARSE_OK</code> lines. But I redi=
rected child stdout to nul inside tests.c. So why do those appear?</p>
<p>OH. The child tools print to stdout, which I redirected to "nul" via fre=
open. But the <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">frame written</code> and <code=
 class=3D"cr-inline-code">PARSE_OK</code> lines appear in the SECOND comman=
d's stderr capture (2&gt;&amp;1). That means those lines are printed to STD=
ERR by the child tools, not stdout! So my freopen("nul",stdout) didn't help=
 because the child writes to its own stdout which is the same as parent's o=
riginal stdout (fd 1). Hmm, but I redirected parent's stdout (fd 1) to nul,=
 so child's fd 1 should also be nul after the _dup/_dup2...</p>
<p>Actually the issue: <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">_spawnl</code> with <=
code class=3D"cr-inline-code">_P_WAIT</code> =E2=80=94 the child inherits t=
he parent's stdout handle at spawn time. I do <code class=3D"cr-inline-code=
">freopen("nul","w",stdout)</code> which changes where the parent's stdout =
FILE* writes, but the underlying OS handle (fd 1 / HANDLE) =E2=80=94 freope=
n to "nul" reopens fd 1 to nul. So child should get nul. But the lines stil=
l appear. That means the child tools write <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">P=
ARSE_OK</code>/<code class=3D"cr-inline-code">frame written</code> to stder=
r (fd 2), not stdout. So they're not affected by my stdout redirect. That's=
 why they appear. OK so those are harmless stderr noise from children; my J=
SON summary on stdout is clean (the JSON shows correctly). Good =E2=80=94 t=
he stdout pollution is solved for the JSON (simcheck's <code class=3D"cr-in=
line-code">{"ok":...}</code> JSON went to stdout but now to nul).</p>
<p>Now back to DBG_CATS not appearing. The grep returned nothing in both co=
mmands. But the program clearly reached the emit (JSON printed). So why no =
DBG_CATS?</p>
<p>Unless the build didn't recompile (cached object). The <code class=3D"cr=
-inline-code">bash tools/build_tests.sh</code> first checks if sim.o exists=
 etc. and rebuilds core only if missing. It DOES recompile tests.o always (=
line 17: compiles tests.c every time). So my edit is included.</p>
<p>Wait =E2=80=94 maybe the DBG_CATS fprintf is there but <code class=3D"cr=
-inline-code">grep DBG</code> =E2=80=94 the line is <code class=3D"cr-inlin=
e-code">DBG_CATS: parse_roundtrip=3D502 ...</code>. grep "DBG" should match=
 "DBG_CATS". Unless stderr is buffered and the program crashed/exited befor=
e flush? No, fprintf to stderr is unbuffered by default.</p>
<p>Hmm, but in the second command, <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">./build/t=
ests.exe 2&gt;&amp;1 1&gt;/dev/null | grep DBG</code> =E2=80=94 here <code =
class=3D"cr-inline-code">2&gt;&amp;1</code> makes stderr go to the pipe's s=
tdout-equivalent (terminal), and <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">1&gt;/dev/n=
ull</code> sends stdout to nul. grep reads stdin (the pipe). But stderr was=
 redirected to the original stdout BEFORE <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">1&=
gt;/dev/null</code>? Order: <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">2&gt;&amp;1</cod=
e> then <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">1&gt;/dev/null</code>. After <code c=
lass=3D"cr-inline-code">2&gt;&amp;1</code>, stderr=E2=86=92current stdout (=
terminal/pipe). Then <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">1&gt;/dev/null</code> r=
edirects the file descriptor 1 to nul, but stderr was already dup'd to poin=
t at the old fd1 target. In a pipe context, <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">=
cmd 2&gt;&amp;1 1&gt;/dev/null | grep</code>: stderr goes to the pipe (grep=
), stdout goes to nul. So grep should see DBG_CATS on stderr. It didn't.</p=
>
<p>This is strange. Let me just run and capture to a file fully.</p>
<p>Actually, maybe the program is exiting via <code class=3D"cr-inline-code=
">return g_fail?1:0;</code> but the DBG_CATS is printed before return. Let =
me check: maybe my edit to add DBG_CATS didn't get saved because of the ear=
lier "file modified since read" =E2=80=94 but I did successfully edit. Let =
me verify the DBG_CATS line exists.</p>
<p>Let me grep tests.c for DBG<span>_</span><span>C</span><span>A</span><sp=
an>T</span><span>S</span><span>.</span></p>
