r-md-list-padding-left: 1.29em;"><p>Now the locscan.c utility. It reads a c=
onfig (JSON or YAML), walks the project, counts LOC per category, exclusion=
s, binary detection, and outputs human + JSON.</p>
<p>Let me design locscan.c:</p>
<p>Config fields (from doc 11.9):</p>
<ul>
<li>include_extensions: list of strings</li>
<li>exclude_extensions: list of strings</li>
<li>exclude_paths: list of glob-like patterns</li>
<li>category_rules: ordered rules mapping pattern/extension to category</li=
>
<li>follow_symlinks: bool</li>
<li>max_file_bytes: int</li>
</ul>
<p>For simplicity, I'll support:</p>
<ul>
<li>category_rules: list of objects { "pattern": "*.c" or "src/**", "catego=
ry": "source" } OR { "extension": "c", "category":"source" }. I'll support =
both <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">extension</code> and <code class=3D"cr-=
inline-code">pattern</code> keys. Pattern matching: I'll support simple <co=
de class=3D"cr-inline-code">*.ext</code> and <code class=3D"cr-inline-code"=
>**</code> and substring. To keep robust, I'll implement fnmatch-like for <=
code class=3D"cr-inline-code">*</code> and <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">?=
</code> plus plain substring for directory patterns like <code class=3D"cr-=
inline-code">build/</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Behavior:</p>
<ul>
<li>Walk root recursively (skip directories matching exclude_paths; skip fi=
les with exclude_extensions; skip files &gt; max_file_bytes; skip binary fi=
les via NUL heuristic).</li>
<li>For each non-excluded file, determine category by first matching catego=
ry_rules (in order). Files with include_extensions: if include_extensions n=
on-empty, only count files whose ext is in include. Actually the spec: incl=
ude_extensions lists which to count. I'll implement: if include_extensions =
present and non-empty, only count files whose extension is in it; else coun=
t all non-excluded.</li>
<li>Count physical lines, blank lines, nonblank.</li>
<li>Excluded files counted separately by file count.</li>
<li>Output human table + JSON if --json.</li>
</ul>
<p>I need a directory walker. On Windows/MinGW, use &lt;dirent.h&gt; (MinGW=
 provides it) or FindFirstFile. Since this is compiled with MinGW for Windo=
ws, I'll use FindFirstFile/FindNextFile (the Windows API) for portability, =
but that's verbose. MinGW provides &lt;dirent.h&gt; via its own. Let me use=
 opendir/readdir (MinGW has it). I'll use dirent.h.</p>
<p>Let me write locscan.c. I'll keep it focused.</p>
<p>For "root path": locscan takes a path argument; default ".". Config path=
: <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">.locscan.json</code> or <code class=3D"cr-=
inline-code">.locscan.yaml</code> or via --config.</p>
<p>Let me write it. I'll implement glob/fnmatch with <code class=3D"cr-inli=
ne-code">*</code> and <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">?</code> and <code cla=
ss=3D"cr-inline-code">**</code>. For exclude_paths patterns like <code clas=
s=3D"cr-inline-code">.git/</code>, <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">build/</c=
ode>, <code class=3D"cr-inline-code">out/</code>, I'll do: if pattern ends =
with '/', treat as directory prefix match (path starts with pattern). Else =
fnmatch.</p>
<p>Let me write locscan.c.</p>
<p>Given the complexity, I'll implement a reasonably complete version.</p>
<p>Actually, to manage scope and risk, let me implement locscan with these =
supported config keys: include_extensions, exclude_extensions, exclude_path=
s, category_rules (list of {extension|pattern, category}), follow_symlinks,=
 max_file_bytes. And the walker. Output human + JSON.</p>
